examples.question_text:
The graph shows the parabola $y=f(x)$. State where its derivative is negative, zero and positive, then sketch $y=f'(x)$. A qualitative sketch is sufficient.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-1,0)--(4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-1.6)--(0,3.5) node[above] {$f(x)$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-0.1:4.1,samples=81] plot (\x,{(\x-2)*(\x-2)-1});
\draw[black,dashed] (2,0)--(2,-1);
\fill[black] (2,-1) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (2,0) {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The parabola decreases before $x=2$, has a horizontal tangent at $x=2$, and increases afterwards.

For $x<2$, $f'(x)<0$. At $x=2$, $f'(2)=0$. For $x>2$, $f'(x)>0$.

The derivative of a quadratic is linear. Sketch a line with positive gradient crossing the $x$-axis at $(2,0)$. The given qualitative graph does not specify its numerical gradient.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curvered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-1,0)--(4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-2.5)--(0,2.5) node[above] {$f'(x)$};
\draw[curvered,thick,<->] (-0.2,-2.2)--(4.2,2.2);
\fill[black] (2,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (2,0) {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The cubic $y=f(x)$ has turning points at $x=-2$ and $x=2$. Sketch its derivative, showing the intercepts and signs. A qualitative sketch is sufficient.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-3.6,0)--(3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above] {$f(x)$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-3.4:3.4,samples=121] plot (\x,{0.15*(\x*\x*\x-12*\x)});
\draw[black,dashed] (-2,0)--(-2,2.4);
\draw[black,dashed] (2,0)--(2,-2.4);
\fill[black] (-2,2.4) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[black] (2,-2.4) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-2,0) {$-2$};
\node[above,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (2,0) {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The horizontal tangents give $f'(-2)=0$ and $f'(2)=0$.

The curve increases for $x<-2$ and $x>2$, so the derivative is positive there. It decreases for $-2<x<2$, so the derivative is negative there.

The derivative of a cubic is quadratic. Sketch an upward-opening parabola crossing the $x$-axis at $-2$ and $2$. No numerical vertical scale is required.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curvered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-3.5,0)--(3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-2.5)--(0,3) node[above] {$f'(x)$};
\draw[curvered,thick,<->,domain=-3.1:3.1,samples=81] plot (\x,{0.5*(\x*\x-4)});
\fill[black] (-2,0) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[black] (2,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-2,0) {$-2$};
\node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (2,0) {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The cubic $y=f(x)$ has a horizontal point of inflection at $(1,-1)$. Its tangent gradient is positive everywhere else. Sketch $y=f'(x)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-1,0)--(3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-3)--(0,1.5) node[above] {$f(x)$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-0.5:2.5,samples=101] plot (\x,{0.5*(\x-1)*(\x-1)*(\x-1)-1});
\fill[black] (1,-1) circle (1.5pt) node[below right] {$(1,-1)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The horizontal tangent gives $f'(1)=0$. The positive gradients on both sides place the derivative above the $x$-axis for $x\ne1$.

The cubic's gradient decreases to zero, then increases. Its quadratic derivative therefore touches the $x$-axis at its vertex $(1,0)$ and opens upwards.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curvered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-1,0)--(3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-0.7)--(0,3) node[above] {$f'(x)$};
\draw[curvered,thick,<->,domain=-0.6:2.6,samples=81] plot (\x,{(\x-1)*(\x-1)});
\fill[black] (1,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (1,0) {$1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The quartic $y=f(x)$ has local minima at $x=-1$ and $x=3$, and a local maximum at $x=1$. Sketch $y=f'(x)$, showing its intercepts and signs.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-2.2,0)--(4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-1.7)--(0,2.2) node[above] {$f(x)$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-1.85:3.85,samples=161] plot (\x,{0.15*((\x-1)*(\x-1)-4)*((\x-1)*(\x-1)-4)-1});
\draw[black,dashed] (-1,0)--(-1,-1);
\draw[black,dashed] (1,0)--(1,1.4);
\draw[black,dashed] (3,0)--(3,-1);
\fill[black] (-1,-1) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[black] (1,1.4) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[black] (3,-1) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (1,0) {$1$};
\node[above,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (3,0) {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The stationary points give derivative zeros at $x=-1,1,3$.

The curve decreases for $x<-1$ and $1<x<3$, so $f'(x)<0$ there. It increases for $-1<x<1$ and $x>3$, so $f'(x)>0$ there.

The derivative of a quartic is cubic. It crosses from negative to positive at each minimum and from positive to negative at the maximum.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curvered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-2.2,0)--(4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-2.3)--(0,2.3) node[above] {$f'(x)$};
\draw[curvered,thick,<->,domain=-1.6:3.6,samples=141] plot (\x,{0.3*(\x+1)*(\x-1)*(\x-3)});
\fill[black] (-1,0) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[black] (1,0) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[black] (3,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\node[above right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (1,0) {$1$};
\node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (3,0) {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The graph of $y=f(x)$ has one horizontal tangent, at its maximum where $x=1$. On each side, the tangent becomes steeper away from the maximum before flattening towards $y=0$; its gradient tends to zero at both ends. Sketch $y=f'(x)$ qualitatively.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-3,0)--(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-0.6)--(0,2.8) node[above] {$f(x)$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-2.7:4.7,samples=141] plot (\x,{2/(1+(\x-1)*(\x-1))});
\draw[black,dashed] (1,0)--(1,2);
\fill[black] (1,2) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (1,0) {$1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The curve increases for $x<1$, so $f'(x)>0$ there. At its maximum, $f'(1)=0$. It decreases for $x>1$, so $f'(x)<0$ there.

The greatest positive and negative gradient magnitudes occur on the steep sections, away from the maximum. Sketch a positive hump before $x=1$ and a negative trough afterwards.

The given gradient limits make the derivative approach zero from above on the left and from below on the right. The sketch's vertical scale is qualitative.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curvered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-3,0)--(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-1.7)--(0,1.7) node[above] {$f'(x)$};
\draw[curvered,thick,<->,domain=-2.7:4.7,samples=181] plot (\x,{-4*(\x-1)/((1+(\x-1)*(\x-1))*(1+(\x-1)*(\x-1)))});
\fill[black] (1,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (1,0) {$1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The graph of $y=f(x)$ has vertical asymptote $x=2$ and horizontal asymptote $y=0$. On each branch its tangent gradient is negative, tends to negative infinity near $x=2$, and tends to zero far from the asymptote. Sketch $y=f'(x)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-1.5,0)--(5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above] {$f(x)$};
\draw[black,dashed] (2,-3)--(2,3);
\node[above] at (2,3) {$x=2$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-1.2:1.6,samples=101] plot (\x,{1/(\x-2)});
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=2.4:5.2,samples=101] plot (\x,{1/(\x-2)});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Both branches decrease, and the given tangent gradients are negative everywhere in the domain. The derivative therefore lies below the $x$-axis on both sides of $x=2$, with no zeros.

As $x$ approaches $2$ from either side, $f'(x)$ tends to negative infinity. As $x$ tends to either positive or negative infinity, $f'(x)$ tends to zero from below.

The derivative is undefined at $x=2$. Its qualitative sketch has two branches below the axis, vertical asymptote $x=2$, and horizontal asymptote $y=0$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curvered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-1.5,0)--(5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-3.6)--(0,0.8) node[above] {$f'(x)$};
\draw[black,dashed] (2,-3.6)--(2,0.8);
\node[above] at (2,0.8) {$x=2$};
\draw[curvered,thick,<->,domain=-1.2:1.45,samples=101] plot (\x,{-1/((\x-2)*(\x-2))});
\draw[curvered,thick,<->,domain=2.55:5.2,samples=101] plot (\x,{-1/((\x-2)*(\x-2))});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The graph shown is $y=f'(x)$. State where $f$ increases and decreases, and classify its stationary points.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curvered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-3.5,0)--(4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-2.2)--(0,2.8) node[above] {$f'(x)$};
\draw[curvered,thick,<->,domain=-3:4,samples=101] plot (\x,{-0.4*(\x+2)*(\x-3)});
\fill[black] (-2,0) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[black] (3,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-2,0) {$-2$};
\node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (3,0) {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The graph shown is $y=f'(x)$. Describe the stationary point of $y=f(x)$ at $x=-1$, using both the derivative's sign and how its values change.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curvered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-3.2,0)--(1.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-0.7)--(0,3) node[above] {$f'(x)$};
\draw[curvered,thick,<->,domain=-2.6:0.6,samples=81] plot (\x,{(\x+1)*(\x+1)});
\fill[black] (-1,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Since $f'(-1)=0$, the tangent to $f$ is horizontal at $x=-1$.

The derivative is positive on both sides, so $f$ increases on both sides. There is no change from increasing to decreasing or from decreasing to increasing.

Before $x=-1$, the positive gradient decreases towards zero; afterwards, it increases away from zero. Thus $f$ changes from concave down to concave up and has a horizontal point of inflection at $x=-1$.

A possible original curve is shown below. Its vertical position is illustrative; the derivative does not determine that position.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-3.2,0)--(1.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-1.5)--(0,2.6) node[above] {$f(x)$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-2.6:0.6,samples=101] plot (\x,{(\x+1)*(\x+1)*(\x+1)/3+0.5});
\draw[black,dashed] (-1,0)--(-1,0.5);
\fill[black] (-1,0.5) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
